<h2 id="vds-section">Vehicle Descriptor Section (Positions 4-8)</h2>

<p>The Vehicle Descriptor Section (VDS) occupies positions 4 through 8 of the VIN and provides detailed information about the vehicle's model, body type, engine, safety systems, and other manufacturer-specific attributes. Unlike the WMI, which follows strict international standards, the VDS allows manufacturers significant flexibility in how they encode information. This means that position 4 might represent engine type for one manufacturer and body style for another.</p>

<h3>What the VDS Typically Encodes</h3>

<p>While the exact meaning varies by manufacturer, the VDS generally includes:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Model or Series</strong>: The specific vehicle line (e.g., Civic, Accord, F-150)</li>
  <li><strong>Body Type</strong>: Sedan, coupe, SUV, pickup, wagon, convertible</li>
  <li><strong>Engine Type</strong>: Engine size, number of cylinders, fuel type (gasoline, diesel, electric, hybrid)</li>
  <li><strong>Restraint Systems</strong>: Type and location of airbags and seatbelt systems</li>
  <li><strong>Trim Level</strong>: Base, LX, EX, Limited, etc.</li>
  <li><strong>Transmission</strong>: Manual, automatic, CVT</li>
</ul>

<h3>Manufacturer-Specific VDS Examples</h3>

<p>Because each manufacturer designs its own VDS encoding system, you need manufacturer-specific documentation to fully decode this section. Here are some examples:</p>

<h4>Honda/Acura VDS (Positions 4-8)</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Position 4</strong>: Vehicle line (e.g., 'F' = Civic, 'C' = Accord)</li>
  <li><strong>Position 5</strong>: Series within the line (e.g., 'A' = base, 'B' = DX, 'C' = LX)</li>
  <li><strong>Position 6</strong>: Body type (e.g., '1' = 2-door coupe, '4' = 4-door sedan)</li>
  <li><strong>Position 7</strong>: Restraint system (e.g., '2' = active seatbelts front, '4' = dual airbags)</li>
  <li><strong>Position 8</strong>: Engine type (e.g., '1' = 1.5L, '3' = 2.0L, '7' = 3.5L V6)</li>
</ul>

<h4>Toyota/Lexus VDS (Positions 4-8)</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Position 4</strong>: Vehicle line/series (e.g., 'A' = Camry, 'U' = RAV4)</li>
  <li><strong>Position 5</strong>: Engine family (e.g., 'A' = 2.5L 4-cyl, 'F' = 3.5L V6)</li>
  <li><strong>Position 6</strong>: Body type (e.g., '3' = sedan, '5' = SUV)</li>
  <li><strong>Position 7</strong>: Restraint system type</li>
  <li><strong>Position 8</strong>: Model variant or specific engine code</li>
</ul>

<h4>Ford VDS (Positions 4-8)</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Position 4</strong>: Gross Vehicle Weight Rating (GVWR) and brake system</li>
  <li><strong>Position 5</strong>: Vehicle line (e.g., 'F' = F-Series, 'P' = Passenger car)</li>
  <li><strong>Position 6</strong>: Series (e.g., '1' = F-150, '2' = F-250)</li>
  <li><strong>Position 7</strong>: Body type (e.g., 'W' = pickup, 'L' = sedan)</li>
  <li><strong>Position 8</strong>: Engine type (e.g., 'G' = 3.5L V6 EcoBoost, 'S' = 5.0L V8)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why the VDS Matters</h3>

<p>The VDS is crucial for ordering the correct parts, verifying vehicle specifications, and detecting title-washing schemes. For instance, if a seller claims a vehicle has a V8 engine but the VDS encodes a 4-cylinder engine, you've identified a potential fraud. Similarly, insurance companies use the VDS to assess risk factors based on engine size, body type, and safety features.</p>

<p>To decode the VDS accurately, you'll need to use a manufacturer-specific VIN decoder or reference the automaker's internal documentation. The <a href="/vin-decoder/">Car Alpha VIN Decoder</a> automatically decodes VDS information for all major manufacturers using the latest NHTSA databases and manufacturer specifications. For a complete explanation of what each VIN position represents across different manufacturers, see our <a href="/learn/vin-positions/">VIN position breakdown guide</a>.</p>

<h2 id="check-digit">The Check Digit (Position 9)</h2>

<p>Position 9 of every VIN is reserved for the check digit—a calculated value that mathematically verifies the rest of the VIN. This security feature was introduced to detect transcription errors and prevent VIN fraud. When someone alters a VIN by changing one or more characters, the check digit will no longer match the calculated value, immediately flagging the VIN as invalid or potentially fraudulent.</p>

<h3>How the Check Digit Algorithm Works</h3>

<p>The check digit is calculated using a standardized algorithm defined in the ISO 3779 and Federal Motor Vehicle Safety Standard (FMVSS) 115. The process involves three steps:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Transliterate letters to numbers</strong>: Convert all alphabetic characters in the VIN (except the check digit itself) to numeric values using a standardized table</li>
  <li><strong>Apply position weights</strong>: Multiply each transliterated value by a weight factor based on its position in the VIN</li>
  <li><strong>Calculate and verify</strong>: Sum all weighted values, divide by 11, and compare the remainder to the check digit</li>
</ol>

