    <tr><td>2</td><td>H</td><td>8</td><td>7</td><td>56</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>G</td><td>7</td><td>6</td><td>42</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>B</td><td>2</td><td>5</td><td>10</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td>H</td><td>8</td><td>4</td><td>32</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6</td><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>3</td><td>12</td></tr>
    <tr><td>7</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr>
    <tr><td>8</td><td>J</td><td>1</td><td>10</td><td>10</td></tr>
    <tr><td>9</td><td>X</td><td colspan="3">Check Digit (skip)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>10</td><td>M</td><td>4</td><td>9</td><td>36</td></tr>
    <tr><td>11</td><td>N</td><td>5</td><td>8</td><td>40</td></tr>
    <tr><td>12</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>7</td><td>7</td></tr>
    <tr><td>13</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>6</td><td>0</td></tr>
    <tr><td>14</td><td>9</td><td>9</td><td>5</td><td>45</td></tr>
    <tr><td>15</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>4</td><td>4</td></tr>
    <tr><td>16</td><td>8</td><td>8</td><td>3</td><td>24</td></tr>
    <tr><td>17</td><td>6</td><td>6</td><td>2</td><td>12</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<ol start="2">
  <li>Sum all products: 8+56+42+10+32+12+2+10+36+40+7+0+45+4+24+12 = <strong>340</strong></li>
  <li>Divide by 11: 340 ÷ 11 = 30 remainder <strong>10</strong></li>
  <li>Check digit representation: If remainder = 10, the check digit is represented as 'X'. Otherwise, use the remainder digit (0-9).</li>
  <li>Compare to position 9: The VIN has 'X' at position 9, which matches our calculated value of 10. The VIN is valid.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Why This Matters for Fraud Detection</h3>

<p>The check digit algorithm is highly sensitive to changes. Altering even a single character in the VIN will almost always result in an incorrect check digit. This makes it extremely difficult for criminals to alter VINs without detection. However, sophisticated VIN cloning operations do recalculate valid check digits after altering other positions, which is why the check digit should always be used in combination with other verification methods.</p>

<p>When you use the <a href="/vin-decoder/">Car Alpha VIN Decoder</a>, the tool automatically validates the check digit before displaying results. If the check digit doesn't match, you'll receive an error message indicating the VIN may be invalid or fraudulent. Always verify the check digit manually when examining physical VIN plates on a vehicle to ensure they haven't been tampered with.</p>

<h2 id="vis-section">Vehicle Identifier Section (Positions 10-17)</h2>

<p>The Vehicle Identifier Section (VIS) occupies the final eight positions of the VIN and provides information that uniquely identifies the specific vehicle. While positions 1-9 tell you what type of vehicle it is, positions 10-17 tell you which exact vehicle it is.</p>

<h3>Position 10: Model Year</h3>

<p>Position 10 encodes the model year of the vehicle using a repeating 30-year cycle. The system uses letters A-Y (excluding I, O, Q, U, and Z) and numbers 1-9 to represent years. The letter 'A' represents 1980 and 2010, 'B' represents 1981 and 2011, and so on. After 'Y' (2000 or 2030), the cycle returns to '1' (2001 or 2031), then continues with numbers through '9' (2009 or 2039) before starting back at 'A'.</p>

